    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking at some data for a new low-fat mayo formulation and hitting some stability issues in our accelerated aging tests. The pH is quite low (around 3.5) and we're seeing significant phase separation after 48 hours. Can you walk me through the specific role lecithin plays in stabilizing the oil-water interface in these high-acid environments?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In a low-fat mayo formulation, lecithin is an excellent emulsifier that helps stabilize the oil-water interface, preventing phase separation. Lecithin, particularly soy lecithin, has both surface-active and self-emulsifying properties. In acidic environments, lecithin's surface-active properties allow it to adhere more strongly to the particle surfaces, reducing the formation of hydrogen bonds between the lecithin and acid, thereby maintaining a more stable interface. This helps to emulsify theensions, keeping them suspended in the water phase.",
      "source": "target",
